## Why This Is Asked
This question tests offline reasoning for dynamic bipartiteness under batched updates, along with the ability to combine DSU with rollback and time-block decomposition.

## Key Concepts
- **Parity (Bipartiteness) DSU**: Maintains connected components with parity labels to detect odd cycles, enabling O(α(N)) union-find operations.
- **Rollback / Persistent State**: Allows reverting DSU state within a block to test multiple edge configurations efficiently.
- **Time-based Block Decomposition**: Divides updates into blocks, rebuilding the DSU at block boundaries while processing updates within blocks using rollback.
- **Trade-offs**: Balancing rebuild cost (at block boundaries) against per-update cost (within blocks).

## Impact of Batch Size B
- **Larger B**: More edges processed per batch → fewer rebuilds → lower amortized cost per edge, but higher memory overhead for rollback structures.
- **Smaller B**: More frequent rebuilds → higher amortized cost, but lower memory since fewer rollback checkpoints are needed.
- **Optimal B**: Typically Θ(√M) balances O(√M · α(N)) per batch with O(M) space.

## Code Example
```cpp
struct ParityDSU {
    vector<int> parent, rank, parity;
    struct Change { int u, oldParent, oldRank, oldParity; };
    vector<Change> stack;

    ParityDSU(int n) : parent(n), rank(n, 0), parity(n, 0) {
        iota(parent.begin(), parent.end(), 0);
    }

    int find(int x) {
        if (parent[x] == x) return x;
        return find(parent[x]);
    }

    int findWithParity(int x) {
        if (parent[x] == x) return x;
        int p = findWithParity(parent[x]);
        parity[x] ^= parity[parent[x]];
        return parent[x] = p;
    }

    bool unite(int x, int y) {
        x = findWithParity(x);
        y = findWithParity(y);
        if (x == y) return parity[x] != parity[y];
        if (rank[x] < rank[y]) swap(x, y);
        stack.push_back({y, parent[y], rank[y], parity[y]});
        parent[y] = x;
        if (rank[x] == rank[y]) rank[x]++;
        parity[y] = parity[x] ^ parity[y] ^ 1;
        return true;
    }

    void snapshot() { stack.clear(); }
    void rollback() {
        while (!stack.empty()) {
            auto c = stack.back(); stack.pop_back();
            parent[c.u] = c.oldParent;
            rank[c.u] = c.oldRank;
            parity[c.u] = c.oldParity;
        }
    }
};
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests understanding of dynamic connectivity under incremental edge insertions and practical amortized analysis in graph algorithms.

## Key Concepts

- Disjoint Set Union (DSU) with path compression and union by size
- Amortized α(n) (inverse Ackermann function) for near-constant operations
- Batch processing efficiency vs full recomputation approaches

## Code Example

```javascript
class DSU {
  constructor(n) {
    this.parent = Array.from({length: n}, (_, i) => i);
    this.size = new Array(n).fill(1);
    this.components = n;
  }

  find(a) {
    if (this.parent[a] !== a) {
      this.parent[a] = this.find(this.parent[a]);
    }
    return this.parent[a];
  }

  union(a, b) {
    const rootA = this.find(a);
    const rootB = this.find(b);
    if (rootA === rootB) return false;

    if (this.size[rootA] < this.size[rootB]) {
      [rootA, rootB] = [rootB, rootA];
    }
    this.parent[rootB] = rootA;
    this.size[rootA] += this.size[rootB];
    this.components--;
    return true;
  }
}

function processBatches(n, batches) {
  const dsu = new DSU(n);
  const results = [];

  for (const batch of batches) {
    for (const [u, v] of batch) {
      dsu.union(u, v);
    }
    results.push(dsu.components);
  }

  return results;
}
```

## Analysis

**Preprocessing:** O(n) time to initialize DSU arrays, O(n) memory for parent and size arrays.

**Per-batch updates:** O(B·α(n)) time for B union operations with path compression and union by size.

**Total complexity:** O(n + m·α(n)) time, O(n) memory.

**Comparison to recomputation:** Full DFS/BFS after each batch would cost O(n + m) per batch, totaling O((n + m)·(m/B)) time—significantly worse for large graphs with many small batches.

The DSU approach leverages amortized near-constant operations, making it ideal for dynamic connectivity scenarios with incremental updates.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests dynamic triangle counting with tight amortized analysis, batching, and memory trade-offs.

## Key Concepts
- Dynamic graphs, incremental triangles
- Degree-based edge orientation, forward neighbor sets
- Amortized analysis, batch processing, space efficiency

## Code Example
```javascript
// high-level sketch
const Nplus = Array.from({length: n}, ()=> new Set());
const deg = new Int32Array(n);
let triangles = 0;
function addEdge(u,v){
  // decide orientation
  if (deg[u] > deg[v] || (deg[u]===deg[v] && u>v)) [u,v] = [v,u];
  // count new triangles
  let t = 0;
  for (let w of Nplus[u]) if (Nplus[v].has(w)) t++;
  triangles += t;
  Nplus[u].add(v);
  deg[u]++; deg[v]++;
  // potentially rebalance orientation lazily
}
```

